From: Haldexx
Subject: [PATCH] navbar: use the SystemUI bar, not the taskbar, on phones

enable_taskbar_on_phones is on, so supportsTaskbar() is true on noblelte and
initializeTaskbarIfNecessary() removes the SystemUI NavigationBar and shows the
Launcher taskbar instead. On this hardware-key phone the forced navigation bar
is then drawn by the taskbar, whose Back/Home/Recents do nothing when pressed
(touches reach TaskbarManager but no action runs). Stop treating a handheld as
taskbar-eligible so SystemUI creates its own functional 3-button NavigationBar.

---
 packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/navigationbar/NavigationBarControllerImpl.java | 7 +++++--
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/navigationbar/NavigationBarControllerImpl.java b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/navigationbar/NavigationBarControllerImpl.java
--- a/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/navigationbar/NavigationBarControllerImpl.java
+++ b/packages/SystemUI/src/com/android/systemui/navigationbar/NavigationBarControllerImpl.java
@@ -288,9 +288,12 @@
 
     @VisibleForTesting
     boolean supportsTaskbar() {
-        // Enable for tablets, unfolded state on a foldable device, (non handheld AND flag is set),
-        // or handheld when enableTaskbarOnPhones() returns true.
-        boolean foldedOrPhone = !mIsPhone || enableTaskbarOnPhones();
+        // Enable for tablets and the unfolded state on a foldable device. noblelte is a
+        // handheld with hardware keys and no navigation bar overlay; when the user forces
+        // the navigation bar on, use SystemUI's functional 3-button NavigationBar rather
+        // than the Launcher taskbar (whose buttons are inert in this forced case), so
+        // enableTaskbarOnPhones() is intentionally not honoured here.
+        boolean foldedOrPhone = !mIsPhone;
         return mIsLargeScreen || foldedOrPhone;
     }
 
